  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sports and Media as a Tool for Integration and Transformation for Youth &amp; Women  in Nairobi Slums</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">St.  John&apos;s Sports  society&apos;s initiatives are geared  towards  an integral human development through  sports and advocacy with  local  community  in the slum. The society has intergrated both sports society project KEN575  and KEN561 activities  in this  one  project. 
St. John Sports Society is an initiative of St. John&apos;s Christian Community which has served communities in Korogocho beyond the spiritual needs over the last 20 years. Korogocho is a low-income, informal settlement with around 100,000 people located in the Kasarani Constituency of East Nairobi and is divided into nine villages inhabited by people from across all Kenyan communities, with most of them living below the poverty line.  

Founded in 2002, the Network looks at the problems affecting the people in the slums, with the eyes of the Gospel. Kutoka Network  aim is to analyse the reality of the slums, to share experiences, to reflect together on the pastoral approach, to plan common initiatives and actions and to highlight positive aspects of the slum realities. Most of  the ctions lobby the government and other stakeholders to find together with the slums dwellers the solutions to the problems affecting our communities. 

The St. John Sports Society, Korogocho (SSS)&apos;s goal is to promote an integral human development with a focus to developing the sporting potential among the youth and women thereby helping them access opportunities beyond Korogocho.  Its main focus is on human formation and community work. Through SSS&apos;s formation programmes the members develop certain acceptable ethical and societal norms, and qualities which help them integrate with the whole society without difficulties. Values such as teamwork, tolerance, forgiveness and reconciliation, self-esteem and self-discipline, morality, commitment and hard work, zero tolerance to corruption and discrimination of any form are encouraged and developed during the formation programmes. 

The SSS operates on lessons learnt in working in the slums of Korogocho include, that sports has a potential to rehabilitate youths in a slum setting making them reformed and more productive members of the society; and that the slums have tremendously talented sports men and women who only lack exposure  and support.

This is a three year project focused on improving human rights situation  in Korogocho</narrative>
  </description>
